WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.523
"Here you go, Adriana."

2
00:00:02.524 --> 00:00:05.876
Grandpa handed me an apron and a cap.

3
00:00:05.877 --> 00:00:09.834
They looked silly with red, white, and green stripes.

4
00:00:09.835 --> 00:00:15.055
I gulped. Did I really have to wear them?

5
00:00:15.056 --> 00:00:18.140
What if someone from school saw me?

6
00:00:18.141 --> 00:00:22.981
I didn't want to hurt Grandpa's feelings.

7
00:00:22.982 --> 00:00:28.599
He had spent the past year getting his pizzeria ready for business.

8
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:34.659
Years ago he had been a pizzaiolo —a pizza maker—in

9
00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:40.466
Italy. Now he was finally going to make authentic Italian pizza here in

10
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:42.636
America. His dream was coming

11
00:00:42.637 --> 00:00:49.802
true. But all I saw was a fashion

12
00:00:49.803 --> 00:00:56.759
nightmare. "Pass out free slices of pizza Margherita to everyone who walks by," Grandpa

13
00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.546
instructed. "And be sure to tell them the

14
00:01:00.547 --> 00:01:07.743
story." I grabbed a tray and started passing out pizza slices to people from a table set up outside.

15
00:01:07.744 --> 00:01:15.249
Then I saw them: Rachel and Clara—the most popular girls at my school.

16
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.347
I pulled down my cap to hide my face.

17
00:01:18.348 --> 00:01:20.829
But it was too late.

18
00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:25.561
"Hey, you're Adriana, from school, right?" Rachel said.

19
00:01:25.562 --> 00:01:30.109
"I didn't know there was a new pizza place in town."

20
00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:34.504
Clara was looking around.

21
00:01:34.505 --> 00:01:38.079
"Don't you have pepperoni or Hawaiian pizza?"

22
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.139
"Oh! Hi!" I blurted out.

23
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.922
I lifted my cap and started talking—much too fast.

24
00:01:46.923 --> 00:01:48.704
"Yes, my grandpa owns this place.

25
00:01:48.705 --> 00:01:52.564
Today's our grand opening.

26
00:01:52.565 --> 00:01:55.886
We don't serve American-style pizza, like pepperoni or Hawaiian.

27
00:01:55.887 --> 00:01:58.505
We only have pizza like they do in Italy.

28
00:01:58.506 --> 00:02:00.865
My grandpa wants it to be authentic."

29
00:02:00.866 --> 00:02:03.390
"Awesome," Rachel said.

30
00:02:03.391 --> 00:02:05.730
"Can we try some?"

31
00:02:05.731 --> 00:02:09.142
"Sure." I tried to act cool.

32
00:02:09.143 --> 00:02:17.140
I put two slices on plates and gave them to the girls.

33
00:02:17.141 --> 00:02:21.055
"Mmm. Delicious!" Clara said.

34
00:02:21.056 --> 00:02:23.745
Rachel nodded.

35
00:02:23.746 --> 00:02:27.865
"Much better than pepperoni pizza! What is this called?"

36
00:02:27.866 --> 00:02:31.766
"It's called pizza Margherita.

37
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:35.093
It was named after Queen Margherita.

38
00:02:35.094 --> 00:02:41.587
Back in 1889 in Italy, pizza was eaten mostly by workers who needed a quick lunch.

39
00:02:41.588 --> 00:02:44.522
But the queen was tired of fancy food.

40
00:02:44.523 --> 00:02:48.528
So she asked a pizza maker to make some pizzas.

41
00:02:48.529 --> 00:02:55.322
He made one with tomatoes, mozzarella cheese, and basil—the colors of the Italian flag!

42
00:02:55.323 --> 00:03:01.414
The queen loved it, and the pizza maker named the pizza after her.

43
00:03:01.415 --> 00:03:06.590
Soon everybody in Italy started eating pizza."

44
00:03:06.591 --> 00:03:12.150
Rachel grinned. "We owe a big thanks to that Italian queen!" "Can

45
00:03:12.151 --> 00:03:16.355
we try some other types of pizza?" Clara asked. I

46
00:03:16.356 --> 00:03:22.892
got some more slices from the kitchen. "This

47
00:03:22.893 --> 00:03:26.516
is pizza bianca ," I said.

48
00:03:26.517 --> 00:03:27.625
"It's from Rome.

49
00:03:27.626 --> 00:03:31.869
There's no cheese—just olive oil and salt."

50
00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:39.680
Next the girls tried the Sicilian-style pizza with anchovies—and they liked that too!

51
00:03:39.681 --> 00:03:44.004
"My family is Italian American too," Rachel said.

52
00:03:44.005 --> 00:03:48.296
"But no one is good at cooking.

53
00:03:48.297 --> 00:03:52.631
You're so lucky your grandpa makes such great food."

54
00:03:52.632 --> 00:03:56.984
I smiled.

55
00:03:56.985 --> 00:04:01.360
I felt proud of my family's culture—and Grandpa.

56
00:04:01.361 --> 00:04:07.735
When the girls left, I went back inside to get more of Grandpa's delicious pizza.

57
00:04:07.736 --> 00:04:15.757
And I didn’t even care about the apron or the cap anymore.

